  "learn": {
   "exam": [
    {
     "text": "You must _state and use the formula_: magnification = image size ÷ actual size.",
     "group": "mag"
    },
    {
     "head": "Magnification",
     "text": "Magnification is how many times larger the image is than the real specimen. A magnification has no unit: it is one length divided by another length in the same unit, so the units cancel. Write it with the × sign and no unit, for example ×400."
    },
    {
     "head": "Image size",
     "group": "image",
     "text": "The image size is the length on the drawing or the photograph. Measure it with a ruler, usually in millimetres (mm)."
    },
    {
     "head": "Actual size",
     "group": "actual",
     "text": "The actual size is the real length of the specimen. Rearrange the formula to find it: actual size = image size ÷ magnification. To find an image size: image size = actual size × magnification."
    },
    {
     "head": "The same unit for both lengths",
     "text": "Before you divide, _write both lengths in the same unit_, so that the units cancel: mm ÷ mm, or µm ÷ µm. Core questions give lengths in millimetres (mm). Supplement questions also use micrometres (µm)."
    },
    {
     "head": "Show your working",
     "text": "Write the formula, substitute the numbers with their units, then give the answer. Round it as the question asks, for example to 3 significant figures, and give the unit with an actual size or an image size."
    },
    {
     "ext": true,
     "head": "Scale bars",
     "group": "bar",
     "text": "Many micrographs show a scale bar instead of a magnification: a short line, with the real length it stands for written beside it, for example 50 µm. Measure the bar on the page with a ruler, in mm. Then magnification = length of the scale bar ÷ the real length it shows, with both lengths in the same unit."
    },
    {
     "ext": true,
     "head": "Measure it yourself",
     "group": "bar",
     "text": "Press \"Measure a micrograph\" above the picture. Put the ruler's 0 mark on one end of what you measure, then turn the ruler until its edge touches the other end. A length is the longest distance across a structure. A width is measured at right angles (90°) to the length."
    }
   ],
   "golden": [
    "Never write a unit after a magnification: \"×400 mm\" can lose the mark.",
    "Give a measurement in the unit the question asks for, usually millimetres: 4.5 cm is 45 mm. Giving centimetres when the question asks for millimetres is one of the commonest errors in the practical papers.",
    "Divide in the right order: image size ÷ actual size. For a cell, the magnification is larger than 1. If your answer is smaller than 1, you have divided in the wrong order."
   ],
   "examFocus": [
    {
     "tag": "State",
     "text": "magnification = image size ÷ actual size."
    },
    {
     "tag": "Calculate",
     "text": "by writing the formula, substituting the numbers with their units, then giving the answer, rounded as the question asks."
    },
    {
     "tag": "Measure",
     "text": "the line on the photograph with a ruler, in the unit the question asks for (usually mm)."
    }
   ],
   "further": [
    {
     "ref": "2.2.3 Supplement",
     "text": "Cells are often measured in micrometres (µm), and 1 mm = 1000 µm. If one length is in mm and the other in µm, change one of them first, so that both lengths are in the same unit."
    },
    {
     "ref": "Practical skills",
     "text": "A drawing has a magnification too: the length on your drawing ÷ the actual length of the specimen."
    }
   ]
  },
